WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.065
Lesson 2.0.

00:00:02.065 --> 00:00:03.420
Mastery Starter.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.265
New to trading on-chain?

00:00:05.265 --> 00:00:06.325
Start here.

00:00:06.325 --> 00:00:08.932
In about 10 minutes, you'll have the five words

00:00:08.932 --> 00:00:11.485
and the first move this whole module builds on.

00:00:12.785 --> 00:00:14.075
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.075 --> 00:00:17.400
First, the sixty second version of this whole module.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.630
Second, five words you'll hear constantly from here on.

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:22.324
Third, one safe first move you

00:00:22.324 --> 00:00:24.865
can make today, without risking anything.

00:00:24.865 --> 00:00:27.368
And finally, the mastery ladder that shows

00:00:27.368 --> 00:00:29.230
where this module is taking you.

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:33.292
Every swap you'll ever make on-chain moves a

00:00:33.292 --> 00:00:36.170
price that isn't set by any single seller.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:39.167
Trade blind to that, and the pool itself quietly

00:00:39.167 --> 00:00:41.858
charges you for the size of your own trade,

00:00:41.858 --> 00:00:44.060
before a single fee is even counted.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:46.785
The 60-second version.

00:00:48.085 --> 00:00:49.585
Here it is, in full.

00:00:49.585 --> 00:00:52.875
A swap on a decentralised exchange trades against

00:00:52.875 --> 00:00:55.441
a pool of tokens, priced by a formula,

00:00:55.441 --> 00:00:57.715
not by a company's order book.

00:00:57.715 --> 00:01:00.198
Providing liquidity to that same pool earns

00:01:00.198 --> 00:01:02.303
you a share of its trading fees.

00:01:02.303 --> 00:01:04.817
But it also changes what you hold, compared

00:01:04.817 --> 00:01:07.403
to just holding the two tokens yourself.

00:01:07.403 --> 00:01:09.566
This module teaches you to do both,

00:01:09.566 --> 00:01:11.428
knowing the real cost going in.

00:01:12.728 --> 00:01:14.771
Watch what actually happens when you

00:01:14.771 --> 00:01:16.893
tap “swap.” You submit the trade.

00:01:16.893 --> 00:01:19.367
An aggregator checks prices across many

00:01:19.367 --> 00:01:21.398
pools at once, not just one.

00:01:21.398 --> 00:01:24.083
It routes your trade to whichever pool, or split

00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:26.903
of pools, gives you the best price after fees.

00:01:26.903 --> 00:01:29.070
That pool's own formula sets the

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:31.668
exact price as your trade executes.

00:01:31.668 --> 00:01:33.633
And you receive the output token,

00:01:33.633 --> 00:01:35.888
at whatever price the formula produced.

00:01:37.188 --> 00:01:38.278
Quick check.

00:01:38.278 --> 00:01:40.028
What does providing liquidity to

00:01:40.028 --> 00:01:41.353
a pool actually earn you?

00:01:45.773 --> 00:01:48.276
The answer: a share of that pool's trading

00:01:48.276 --> 00:01:50.895
fees, in exchange for changing what you hold

00:01:50.895 --> 00:01:53.863
compared to simply holding the two tokens yourself.

00:01:55.163 --> 00:01:56.468
Five words you'll need.

00:01:57.768 --> 00:02:00.363
The first two describe where a trade happens.

00:02:00.363 --> 00:02:02.916
A DEX, a decentralised exchange, is one

00:02:02.916 --> 00:02:05.404
you use straight from your own wallet,

00:02:05.404 --> 00:02:07.938
no account, no custody handed over.

00:02:07.938 --> 00:02:10.454
A liquidity pool is the pot of two tokens

00:02:10.454 --> 00:02:13.868
sitting inside it that your trade actually swaps against.

00:02:15.168 --> 00:02:17.568
Put real numbers on price impact.

00:02:17.568 --> 00:02:19.591
In a typical pool, a tiny trade

00:02:19.591 --> 00:02:21.788
barely moves the price at all.

00:02:21.788 --> 00:02:24.298
Trade a size that's a meaningful slice of

00:02:24.298 --> 00:02:26.688
the pool, and the price you get worsens

00:02:26.688 --> 00:02:29.498
fast, well before any fee is even applied.

00:02:29.498 --> 00:02:32.860
That's why the same $20 trade can cost noticeably

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:35.348
more in a small pool than a deep one.

00:02:36.648 --> 00:02:38.203
The next two describe cost,

00:02:38.203 --> 00:02:40.058
and they're easy to confuse.

00:02:40.058 --> 00:02:42.101
Price impact is how much your own

00:02:42.101 --> 00:02:44.565
trade moves the price, purely because of

00:02:44.565 --> 00:02:46.668
its size relative to the pool.

00:02:46.668 --> 00:02:48.720
Slippage is the worst price you've told

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.123
the app you'll accept, your safety limit.

00:02:51.123 --> 00:02:53.836
A big trade has real price impact whether

00:02:53.836 --> 00:02:56.742
you set slippage or not; slippage just stops

00:02:56.742 --> 00:02:59.713
it from executing far worse than you expected.

00:03:01.013 --> 00:03:02.318
The fifth word is the

00:03:02.318 --> 00:03:04.458
one that surprises people most.

00:03:04.458 --> 00:03:07.100
Impermanent loss is how far a liquidity

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:09.874
position lags simply holding the same two

00:03:09.874 --> 00:03:12.618
tokens, once their prices move apart.

00:03:12.618 --> 00:03:14.953
It isn't a fee, and it isn't theft.

00:03:14.953 --> 00:03:18.620
It's the mathematical cost of the formula rebalancing your

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:22.100
pool for you, automatically, every time the price moves.

00:03:23.399 --> 00:03:24.994
Here's the shape of it.

00:03:24.994 --> 00:03:27.518
Deposit into a pool when both tokens are

00:03:27.518 --> 00:03:30.034
worth the same as just holding them.

00:03:30.034 --> 00:03:31.999
Let one token's price run up.

00:03:31.999 --> 00:03:34.769
Simply holding would have captured that whole move.

00:03:34.769 --> 00:03:38.348
The pool, instead, has been automatically selling the

00:03:38.348 --> 00:03:41.331
rising token into the falling one the entire

00:03:41.331 --> 00:03:43.752
way up, so the LP position lags.

00:03:43.752 --> 00:03:46.765
Fees earned can close that gap, or even close

00:03:46.765 --> 00:03:49.712
it entirely, but the gap itself is real, and

00:03:49.712 --> 00:03:52.267
it starts the moment prices move apart.

00:03:53.567 --> 00:03:54.657
Quick check.

00:03:54.657 --> 00:03:57.822
What's the real difference between price impact and slippage?

00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:04.795
The answer: price impact is how much your

00:04:04.795 --> 00:04:07.408
trade moves the price, caused by its size;

00:04:07.408 --> 00:04:10.083
slippage is the worst price you've told the

00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:12.757
app you'll accept, a limit you set yourself.

00:04:14.057 --> 00:04:15.332
Your first safe step.

00:04:16.632 --> 00:04:18.274
Before you start this module

00:04:18.274 --> 00:04:20.217
for real, confirm two things.

00:04:20.217 --> 00:04:22.642
You've completed Modules 0 and 1.

00:04:22.642 --> 00:04:25.618
And you have a little ETH for gas, plus

00:04:25.618 --> 00:04:28.967
some USDC, on a low-fee network, ready to go.

00:04:30.267 --> 00:04:31.862
Here's the move itself.

00:04:31.862 --> 00:04:34.111
Pick a token pair you'd actually

00:04:34.111 --> 00:04:35.842
trade, say $20 of it.

00:04:35.842 --> 00:04:39.722
Quote that exact trade on a single DEX directly.

00:04:39.722 --> 00:04:43.007
Then quote the same trade on an aggregator instead.

00:04:43.007 --> 00:04:46.331
Compare what you'd actually receive after gas, on each.

00:04:46.331 --> 00:04:48.153
Don't execute either yet, just look

00:04:48.153 --> 00:04:49.976
at the difference the routing makes.

00:04:51.277 --> 00:04:52.367
Quick check.

00:04:52.367 --> 00:04:53.704
Why quote the same trade

00:04:53.704 --> 00:04:55.522
both ways before executing either?

00:04:59.942 --> 00:05:03.068
The answer: routing through an aggregator can find a

00:05:03.068 --> 00:05:06.665
better price across several pools, and comparing first shows

00:05:06.665 --> 00:05:09.732
you exactly what that's worth, with nothing at risk.

00:05:11.032 --> 00:05:12.127
The mastery ladder.

00:05:13.427 --> 00:05:16.442
Here's where this module takes you, as three rungs.

00:05:16.442 --> 00:05:20.432
Beginner: small swaps, deliberate slippage, protected routing.

00:05:20.432 --> 00:05:24.295
Practitioner: calculating fee APR and impermanent loss,

00:05:24.295 --> 00:05:27.562
tracking a position against simply holding.

00:05:27.562 --> 00:05:31.946
Master: running limit, TWAP and intent-based orders, with

00:05:31.946 --> 00:05:35.347
written rules for every LP position you open.

00:05:36.647 --> 00:05:39.247
Here's the road ahead, lesson by lesson.

00:05:39.247 --> 00:05:44.467
2.1 covers DEXs, aggregators and routing in full.

00:05:44.467 --> 00:05:48.057
2.3 and 2.4 are providing liquidity, and the

00:05:48.057 --> 00:05:51.707
true P&L once impermanent loss is counted.

00:05:51.707 --> 00:05:54.504
2.5 is MEV, the value extracted from

00:05:54.504 --> 00:05:57.677
your trades if you don't protect them.

00:05:57.677 --> 00:06:00.635
And 2.6 through 2.8 take you into

00:06:00.635 --> 00:06:04.376
limit orders, advanced AMM design, and the

00:06:04.376 --> 00:06:06.812
MEV supply chain end to end.

00:06:08.112 --> 00:06:11.338
You've mastered this module when you can predict a

00:06:11.338 --> 00:06:14.817
swap's real cost, and an L.P. position's likely result

00:06:14.817 --> 00:06:18.422
against simply holding, before you ever enter either one.

00:06:19.722 --> 00:06:21.942
To recap: a swap trades against a

00:06:21.942 --> 00:06:25.599
formula-priced pool, and providing liquidity earns fees

00:06:25.599 --> 00:06:27.597
but changes what you hold.

00:06:27.597 --> 00:06:31.431
Price impact and slippage are different things, and impermanent

00:06:31.431 --> 00:06:34.547
loss is the formula rebalancing you, not a fee.

00:06:34.547 --> 00:06:37.052
And your first move is simply to compare,

00:06:37.052 --> 00:06:39.497
quoted two ways, before you ever execute.

00:06:40.797 --> 00:06:43.212
That's your Mastery Starter for Module Two.

00:06:43.212 --> 00:06:46.818
Next up, Lesson 2.1: DEXs, aggregators, and

00:06:46.818 --> 00:06:50.097
how routing actually decides your price.
